      <h1>A social network for people who don't look at the screen.</h1>
      <p class="status">
        <span class="p-tag p-mono">Rebuilding</span>
        <span class="p-mono">Closed beta done · MVP in development</span>
      </p>
      <p class="standfirst">Accessibility-first, for blind and low-vision people. It
      has not shipped. There is no version number on this page because there is no
      version worth naming yet.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="beats" id="beats">

      <section aria-labelledby="b-problem">
        <h2 id="b-problem">The problem</h2>
        <p>Every social network I have used treats sight as the default and works out
        what to do with everyone else afterward. A sighted designer builds the
        interface first. A screen reader gets added after, translating a screen its
        own builder never had to read.</p>
        <p>Third Eye World reverses that order: a social network, accessibility first,
        where the non-visual interaction is designed before the visual layer that
        follows it. You are testing that claim on this page right now, whether you are
        reading it with your eyes or having it read to you.</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="b-constraint">
        <h2 id="b-constraint">The constraint</h2>
        <p>There is no existing gesture language for a non-visual social network, and
        no precedent from a sighted-first app that carries over cleanly. A social
        network is many interactions, and every one of them had to work without sight,
        with nothing to copy from.</p>
        <blockquote>
          <p>“It's my idea, my build, all my decisions.”</p>
          <cite>The direction is mine to get right or get wrong, which means the
          interaction model was mine to design before anyone could test it and tell me
          what I had missed.</cite>
        </blockquote>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="b-decision">
        <h2 id="b-decision">The decision</h2>
        <p>The call that mattered: put an early beta in front of real testers instead
        of refining it alone for another year. That gets feedback sooner, from the
        people the product is for. It also means testers meet something rough before
        it is ready, and that is the trade I made.</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="b-broke">
        <h2 id="b-broke">What broke</h2>
        <blockquote>
          <p>“The whole design of our first beta was dependent on vision more than we
          liked. The gestures were generic, and navigation was hard and required
          multiple gestures to do something.”</p>
        </blockquote>
        <p>The testers said so, and they were right. In that first beta I borrowed the
        gestures from apps built for people looking at a screen. Doing something simple
        took several gestures instead of one. Once the feedback was in, we started
        rebuilding the design around what did not work.</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="b-outcome">
        <h2 id="b-outcome">The outcome</h2>
        <p>The closed beta test is done. The MVP is under active development. There is
        no version number here and no launch date, because neither exists. Third Eye
        World has not shipped, and saying otherwise would repeat the first beta's
        mistake: presenting something as finished before it works.</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="b-noimg">
        <h2 id="b-noimg">Why there are no screenshots</h2>
        <p>Third Eye World has no product screens worth showing. Putting an invented
        interface on this page would be the exact failure the case study is about. When
        the MVP has a real screen, a real screenshot goes here.</p>
      </section>
